Z||000009||ka4nkf||07-01-2001||10:59 PM||||Pull your E-brake lever up al the way. Put your foot on the pedal brake and hold it down while you release the E-brake. do this several times and see if it adjust the e-brake. if not then then the pads were replaced by someone that did not know how to correctly install the E-brake lever.<P>Also if it is a state law that you must have a E-brake then Pontiac should be made to fix it. as you recall the recall notice for E-brake fix was for manual shift fiero's only. so what about us with the automatic. the brakes are the same on manual or automatic.<BR>something to think about.||||reg||1
Z||000010||maryjane||07-01-2001||11:41 PM||||alrighty, that person could very well have been me. What exactly would be the correct way to do this? Today I tried to adjust the e brake with the equalizing nut-no success. <BR>The lever on the calipher moves, but the pads do not engage the rotor. pads only move a little. The foot brake works fine. I pulled the calipher but the pads and rotor look ok. Decided to change the pads while I had them off, but couldn't get the piston pushed back far enough to get the new pads on, barely got the old pads back on. I've changed pads,caliphers, and rotors on lots of non-Fieros. What am I doing wrong? I could really use some guidance here. Thanks,<P>------------------<BR>84 SE 2.5<BR>not slow not fast-just halfast||||reg||5
Z||000011||Carrolles||07-02-2001||09:27 AM||||As you apply the e-brake the lever turns a large screw inside the caliper which pushes against the piston to apply the e-brake.  As the pads wear the self adjustment takes up the slack.  When you install new thicker pads you need to make the piston retract into the caliper to allow room for the thicker pads.<P>I know of two ways to do this.<P>One way is to screw the piston back into the caliper with one of the caliper piston tools.  I've never tried this way.<P>The other way (the one that I use) is to remove the parking brake lever from the actuator arm and use a c-clamp to push the piston back into the claiper bore.  You'll have to cover the actuator arm shaft with a socket so it is allowed to turn as the piston is pushed back in.  When it is retracted, install the pads and mount the caliper on the rotor.  Then use the lever arm to screw the actuator arm back into the caliper until you get slight resistance.  Re-install the lever so that the pads are not binding the rotor when the lever is back against the stop (with the e-brake released).  Now adjust the cable so you get around 6 to 8 clicks of the lever ratchet when applying the e-brake.||||reg||1
Z||000012||ka4nkf||07-02-2001||02:59 PM||||If you want to leave the pads on, then remove the E-brake lever and place a screwdriver in the slot of the caliper and push back the piston. hold the piston back with driver and replace E- brake lever as close to stop as possible. then adjust the brakes like I said before. If you get more than 7 or 8 clicks then adjust the equalizer nut on the cable. also I have found it is best to put 2 lug nuts on the rotor to keep it straight.||||reg||1
Z||000013||maryjane||07-02-2001||11:15 PM||||Thanks for the info. Mary Jane, my better half has decided in her infinite wisdom to take it to the shop and have the brakes redone. The brakes have never really felt right on it, and pads wear out too quickly. I always attributted it to her heavy foot, & I'm not talkin about on the brakes. She drives San Angelo like she's at a Grand Prix somewhere. The shop owner confessed he had not worked on a Fiero, but had done a lot of rear disc brake jobs. Oh well, can't argue her out of this one so I will print out your replies and take the manual and printouts to him in case he has surprises. Thanks again.<BR>Don & Jane  ||||reg||14
Z||000014||mrgone||07-03-2001||01:33 AM||||my emergency brake isnt the greatest, but it somewhat holds the car.<P>my car has a recall sticker on the trunk, so pontiac may have done the e-brake recall. even if they did would it be still recall repair for them to fix it again? or would i have to open my wallet.<BR>if i cant get it for free, my car can roll for all i care  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000015||Carrolles||07-03-2001||09:18 AM||||I would not recommend relying solely on engine compression to keep a standard transmission car parked.  If you are parked on even the slightest incline the car will eventually roll.  Cylinders leak down with time and the engine will turn over with the strain.  You can exaggerate this phenomenon by parking with the trans in fourth or fifth gear.  It will roll in a short time.  Parking with the trans in first or reverse gives you the mechanical advantage of the gearing to hold the car put but it will eventually roll.<P>Does the recall sticker mention the recall campaign number?  It may have been for something different than the e-brake.<P>I bought my 85SE v-6 4spd in '97 with 109000 miles on it.  I called Pontiac and found that the e-brake recall had never been done.  They did the recall for free and the e-brake has worked perfectly since then.  It works as good as any parking brake I've used on any other car.||||reg||1
